# chktex configuration for aa52082-24.
#
# Adjudicated against the ChkTeX manual's own definitions (texmf-dist/doc/chktex/ChkTeX.pdf)
# and against the real sites in aanda.tex. The manual distinguishes two mechanisms and this
# file uses both for what each is for: a resource file for policy that holds document-wide,
# and "% chktex NN" line comments for individual intentional constructs, so that a genuine
# instance elsewhere is still caught.
#
# Fixed in the source rather than suppressed, because they were real:
#   n24  space before \label, 26 sites. A page break inside that space can send \pageref to
#        the wrong page. Newline commented out with %.
#   n13  \@ after an uppercase letter that ends a sentence (ASteCA., 2MASS., NUTS., INI.) or
#        precedes a semicolon (HDBSCAN;, PMS;). LaTeX decides spacing from the character
#        before the punctuation and reads uppercase as an abbreviation, suppressing the
#        wider space that period, colon and semicolon should get.
#   n12  "Dr. Robert" -- Dr. is in ChkTeX's own Abbrev list.
#   n2   a missing ~ before a citation, and one bare \cite among 181 \citep/\citet.
#   n8   five table cells using a bare "-" for a dimensionless unit, now \ldots.

# Dashes that are correct as written. DashExcpt matches the dashed string itself.
# 2-012: the Sharpless catalogue designation, Sh 2-012.
# age--metallicity--extinction and wind--wind: coordinate nouns take an n-dash, the
# Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer case the manual gives as the reason this variable exists.
DashExcpt
{
    age--metallicity--extinction
    wind--wind
}

# Warnings that do not apply anywhere in this document.
#
# 35: 37 hits, all $\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$, $T_{\mathrm{max}}$, $m_{\mathrm{lim}}$,
#     $\mathrm{deg}$. \max is an operator carrying operator spacing; inside a subscript the
#     upright text is what is meant and \max would typeset it wrongly.
# 44: a user regex inherited from the system chktexrc asking for booktabs rules. aa.cls
#     tables use \hline and A&A does not require booktabs.
#  3: the manual itself calls this one optional, "for maximum aestethic pleasure".
# 36: the hits are O7V((f))+O7V((f)), the standard spectral classification, and E(B-V).
#  1: \abstract and \nolinenumbers take no argument; the following space is discarded.
CmdLine { -n35 -n44 -n3 -n36 -n1 }

# Warning 13, uppercase letter before a semicolon (HDBSCAN;, and the \date{} placeholder
# "XXXX;" -- "PMS;" was the third site until that sentence was reworded to "PMS analyses" and
# stopped matching). \@ was briefly "fixed" and then reverted on the strength of a measurement:
# typesetting the phrase with and without it gives 138.94467pt both times -- TeX adds extra space
# only from spacefactor 2000, and a semicolon already sets 1500, so \@ moves the stretch and not
# the natural width. At the time this file still had the "% chktex NN" line-comment mechanism
# available for the two sites where \@ would not fit (see the DashExcpt note below for why it
# didn't), so the warning was suppressed per line instead of "fixed" with markup that does
# nothing.
#
# 2026-09-15 (post-acceptance layout pass, editor asked for a clean .tex): every "% chktex NN"
# line comment in this file was removed, including the two covering this warning, because the
# editor's clean-version request reads a stray directive as leftover markup. With that mechanism
# gone, \@ is now applied at both current sites (\date{Received XXXX\@; accepted YYYY} and
# "selected by HDBSCAN\@; the tall blue ellipse...") even though the measurement above still
# holds and it changes nothing on the page: it is harmless where it used to be pointless, and it
# is now the only way left to satisfy chktex at these two sites without a blanket suppression.
# chktex no longer reports warning 13 anywhere in the document; verified after the edit.
#
# One known residual class, left in place on purpose (accepted by content in gate.toml's
# [linters].accepted_dash, not silenced here -- see that file for why).
#
# Warning 8 on "Sh 2-012" (five sites: the abstract, the introduction, twice in Sect. 8, and
# once more nearby) is the Sharpless designation. DashExcpt does not cover it: the manual's
# examples are all alphabetic, and the digit-digit case falls under NumDash, which wants -- for
# a numeric range. The line-comment mechanism was never usable here either -- one site is the
# first block of the structured \abstract and another ends a paragraph whose next line continues
# the argument, so a % would eat the newline and fuse two arguments together, independently of
# whether "% chktex NN" comments exist elsewhere in the file. Relaxing NumDash to { 1 2 } would
# silence it but would also stop catching a genuine "1-10" written for "1--10", which is the more
# valuable check. A second, unrelated warning-8 site was added 2026-09-15: A&A's required
# \keywords{...} syntax separates entries with a spaced " -- ", which chktex reads as a long dash
# that "should" be \emph{---} instead; changing it would violate house style, so it is accepted
# the same way, by content, not suppressed here.
